Before any implementation under /05, present concrete canonical struct declarations, UnlockedData aliases/storage and exact frontend snapshot member types with source-field mappings, and obtain explicit user approval through /05/02's review gate. Audit completion alone is not coding authorization. The subsequent /05/02 coding phase is limited to approved shared groundwork for existing ModelSelection/SessionStats; later domain migrations remain separate goals.

Separate backend state from frontend-owned state before changing its transport. Use backend-owned canonical objects wrapped in runtime::UnlockedData<T> only for backend state that is or may become visible: notably the current active agent receiving the next submitted prompt, model, variant/reasoning selection, provider, directories including workspace root, and used-token statistics. Verify the real backend owners and writers rather than deriving authority from UI labels. Follow ModelSelection and SessionStats: backend writes notify dirtiness; the tui-thread calls foo_refresh() on TuiFrontEnd-owned projections, copies bounded canonical values under read guards, derives presentation values and independently consumes change flags. RefreshResult<T> supports textual and typed values. Session history is backend-owned but explicitly deferred from this copy-based migration as described below.

Pure appearance and interaction state that does not affect backend behavior belongs in src/ava/tui, src/ava/app/tui or another explicitly tui-named file, owned and accessed by the tui-thread without locks or UnlockedData. This includes themes, local previews, layout and draft editing. The backend is unaware of draft text until Enter submits it as std::string. Persistence of a UI preference does not make it backend state. Classify mixed settings by effect: execution reasoning/model variant and attachment authority are backend concerns; rendering preferences are not. Move UI-only responsibilities behind the frontend boundary rather than inventing canonical backend copies for them.

Separate outgoing actions from incoming backend facts. Actions retain explicit success/error/control and permission/correlation semantics; migrated backend facts arrive through canonical projections, not replacement UI bundles. Handle session transitions and stale asynchronous work through explicit identity/generation rules. Remove TuiRuntimeStateSnapshot, TuiSubmitResult::state_snapshot and migrated narrow state-snapshot transfers; do not rename or wrap those bulk bundles wholesale. Frontend-local render/editor/projection snapshots and backend-internal snapshots may remain.

Explicit deferral: preserve existing session-history/transcript/streaming and associated historical-content delivery for now, including necessary existing content metadata and source attribution. Do not remove working history or force the large history collection through copied snapshots/UnlockedData merely to complete this objective. A later separately designed interface should let a frontend access a list/vector-like collection of history objects, including user or agent/model source, text, reasoning when available and timing metadata, without copying the whole history. No collection lifetime, paging or streaming architecture is selected here. Inventory the retained paths precisely; the exception cannot shelter unrelated agent/model/directory/statistics transfers. Preserve history behavior and safety while incrementally deleting superseded in-scope state paths.

Interactive exchanges are also explicitly deferred: backend-requested permission/question popups, plugin UI requests, subagent/control/inspector exchanges, branch confirmations and related request/completion protocols. Identify these interfaces but preserve their old implementation by default, including protocol-specific state and dependencies. Do not force pending requests/decisions into UnlockedData. The user will first design and implement one backend-requested popup requiring a reply and then decide how to generalize it. Partial removal or an unimplemented endpoint requires specifically agreed scope and must not bypass safety or silently remove features. The no-Snapshot/backend-decoupling completion criteria apply to migrated ordinary facts, not these precisely inventoried deferred paths. Normal runtime maintainability and centralization govern the design; exceptional cleanup does not justify a new protocol architecture.

The /models and /scoped-models lists are also deferred interactive popups, not passive current-state displays. Preserve their existing list and action paths; do not add scoped_model_cycle_refresh(), scope snapshots/change flags or eager model-list publication. Keep canonical scoped_model_cycle unchanged, including optional semantics. The existing current-model/provider/reasoning projection remains in scope and reflects committed selection changes, regardless of whether a popup initiated them.

Sidebar and sidebar-drawer fields are live status data, not modal popup content; session ID/path and other approved backend facts displayed there use canonical UnlockedData and frontend DisplayStrings. Backend /commands are backend mutation sources even when initiated by the user; they publish canonical state without frontend-specific update calls. In contrast, the intended on-demand modal model has local candidate values until Apply/OK and no canonical mutation on cancel. Preserve existing deferred interaction behavior rather than assuming backend execution necessarily stops during every modal. Pure UI preferences remain frontend-local.
